00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
hidden amongst all the pain and stress  of studying for exams you might find  that physics can actually be a very  beautiful subject whilst physics at  school is often about meeting standards  pleasing examiners or being competitive  real physics and math beyond that can be  rewarding and interesting i recently  made some videos about the very  stressful university entrance exam from  turkey and there was a fair amount of  despair in the comments today i wanted  to show a positive example of  achievement coming out of turkey and i  wanted to do that through telling the  story of faiza garcia who was a very  successful mathematician and physicist  i find him very inspiring because not  only was he working on one of the  hardest ideas in physics but he also  relentlessly pursued all knowledge art  music poetry and beautiful ideas he made  significant contributions to general  relativity and particle physics where he  found symmetries that he expressed using  group theory  some of his work would inspire what  would go on to be called quantum  chromodynamics and garcet was highly  respected by many of the top physicists  to follow his journey let's start with a  school report card  jose was born in istanbul in april 1921  but attended primary school in paris  this report card is from january 1928  so when he was only six years old but he  was already earning the respect of his  teachers  his conduct and order are said to be  good and for reading writing calculation  and spelling  there is place first written in this  column so i guess he was top of his  class there's also a first for grammar  and for recitation  overall he's said to have been doing  very good later he would return to  turkey and become a boarding student at  galatasaray high school this is one of  the enrolment documents there it's got  his name and the number 39 which was  assigned to him his father's name that  he was born in istanbul in 1921 that  he's a boarding student who entered the  school in 1930. this is stamped  1939 when he would have been in the 11th  grade and here is a picture of him at  that time  here's a copy of phaser's report card  from 1939 to 1940. there's nothing too  scandalous to report here in fact it  looks like he was quite a good student  and fairly well-rounded down this column  we have the subjects and then i believe  these are grades from written exams the  first two columns and then an opinion  note and then the overall passing grades  signed off by the teachers there's a lot  of nines and tens here which i believe  to be the highest  the subjects here are literature history  geography  natural sciences all tens  also straight tends for physics and  chemistry and there's foreign language  philosophy mechanics geometry and  algebra the second page would detail any  penalties but he doesn't seem to have  any and this is the attendance these are  the rows for days off and days late and  it's signed by the school directors and  indicates an overall pass  jose graduated from the science  department of this high school in 1940  at the top of his class  and he was said to be a legendary  student who impressed both local and  foreign teachers  it was in high school that he decided to  dedicate his life to physics  a classmate of garcia said that at  school during evening study hours you  could often find phaser reading  philosophical stories named in search of  lost time  this is a long novel that comes in seven  parts and is said to try to find an  answer to some of life's questions such  as who am i and what am i to make of  this life fazer was also said to often  be examining the artworks of cezanne and  phases teachers would often call him  into the teacher lounge during breaks to  chat with him after graduating high  school gosse attended the istanbul  university and graduated with a degree  in physics and math in 1944.  graduated top of his class he found the  curriculum at the science department to  not be challenging enough so still had  plenty of time to nurture his interests  in poetry and art  however he said i have to learn physics  in spite of everything perhaps  indicating that he thought he could  excel in many different fields but felt  the strongest calling towards physics he  went to imperial college in london for  his phd which he received in 1950 with  his thesis titled application of  quaternions to quantum field  while he was a grad student gosei wrote  a letter to his mother which seems to  have come from a time when he was  struggling to make progress but was  still holding on to his motivations  he says  i am not hanging out at parties as you  assume dear mother i work day and night  i have been holding on to the things  that i want to work on for two years  with all my strength general field  theory and relations between  electromagnetic nissan and electron  fields  what i am currently working on cannot go  beyond formalism but i hope that at  least if the cambridge philosophical  society accepts me i can show my  supervisor that i am not wasting all my  time he'd said that he was trying to get  fundamental equations into quaternion  formalism but that is supervisor thought  it was unnecessary and priggish to focus  on quaternions but taking a look at his  thesis published three years later it  does look like he made some good  progress the abstract says that the  object of the thesis is to show that the  quaternion algebra can be applied  successfully to the discussion and  solution of dirac's equation  and that it can be extended to general  relativity to give an invariant  formulation of field equations much of  his work after this focused on group  theory and its application to particle  physics in reality the work that he did  is very advanced and takes a lot of  prior knowledge to understand where it's  coming from but let me give just the  briefest of introductions to group  theory to kind of set the stage for the  space that he was working in but first i  would like to quickly thank short form  looking for ways to increase how much i  can learn from books i came across short  form who have kindly sponsored this  video  short form provides detailed book  summaries of the best nonfiction books  it's a way to take books you've always  wanted to read and learn the key ideas  to a deeper level i wouldn't otherwise  have time to read a lot of these books  in their entirety their categories cover  many topics including philosophy science  and communication  i think the books on communication are  the most useful for my own personal  growth like this one called quiet about  how introverts can embrace their  personality instead of trying to force  themselves to be extroverted to fit in  short form drops new book guides and  articles every week by joining through  my link short form dot com slash tibby's  you'll get five days of unlimited access  and an additional 20 off the annual  subscription so check it out the link  will be down in the description now back  to the group theory here i have a square  and a circle and just looking at these  two shapes the circle could be said to  be more symmetrical because there are  more ways that you could rotate it for  example and it would still look the same  in physics a symmetry is a feature that  remains unchanged under some  transformation  if that transformation is rotation then  the circle has a lot more symmetry than  the square there are an infinity of ways  that i could rotate the circle and still  be left with what i started with however  for the square there are some rotations  that don't leave it unchanged like this  one here in fact only rotations of 90  degrees or multiples of 90 would be  considered symmetrical if we were to  write down the set of symmetries for the  square we'd have 90 180 270 and 360. but  one of these rotations is special a  rotation by  360 degrees would have us ending up back  where we started which is the same as a  rotation by zero degrees we call this  the identity element since it does  remain unchanged adding any of these  rotations will give you another rotation  in the set for example 90 plus 180 is  equal to 270.  also every rotation here can be undone  if you've done a rotation of 270 and  want to undo it you can do an additional  rotation of 90 because 270 plus 90 is  360 and that's the identity the second  rotation would be considered the inverse  of the first one and an inverse exists  for every single element  all these properties of these rotations  that i have mentioned are actually  properties of what in maths is called a  group and this set of rotations is just  one example of a group the symmetries of  the circle are also a group but this  time the elements of the group are  infinite and correspond to each possible  angle of rotation in particle physics  there is a group called su-3 which is  very similar to the symmetries of a  sphere and physicists have used  representations of this group to come up  with structures of subatomic particles  there is a lot of symmetry that exists  within the standard model and often  these strange new particles are  predicted before we find them in the lab  because we can see there is a gap or  something missing in the symmetry if you  want to go further into this i'll link  to the ideas of league groups down in  the description  but this was all to show you the basic  idea which faiza gerzi was working in  and the kinds of topics that he was  expanding with his work group theory is  one of the many areas in physics where  you can follow this quote  beauty is truth truth beauty in the  sense that pursuing symmetries and what  seems to work to make the equations  simple and beautiful can lead you to  what is true it isn't always the case  but often it seems to lead you in the  right direction some of faisa girzi's  most important contributions include  this paper  on the symmetries of strong and weak  interactions published in 1960 he also  proposed something called su-6 symmetry  for baryons and mesons he also applied  group theory to scattering of particles  and to general relativity after guerzee  died in 1992  ed whitten wrote about him and said that  he was one of the most respected members  of the physics community he said that  faiza's scientific work is marked with  remarkable originality and elegance as  well as intellectual courage he never  hesitated to pick problems that were not  fashionable he worked at them in depth  planting seeds that in some cases  developed into whole branches of our  discipline he continues that subsequent  attempts to understand phaser's work on  the su-6 symmetry led to the  introduction of the colour degrees of  freedom of quarks  edwitton continued to describe fazer as  a very cultured man  one that you could have very deep  discussions with on the topics of music  poetry  novels and paintings  in short on essentially any subject of  depth and beauty  i hadn't known about phaser gase until  recently  and so i hope sharing his story helps a  few more people to know about him but  also to provide a bit of motivation for  turkish students especially studying for  tough exams  but i also find his story to be a  reminder to stay well-rounded  physics after all is about the world  around us and understanding it  not just about passing exams  thanks short form and thank you for  watching this video a special shout out  to today's patreon cats of the day leo  and misha  [Music]  you

